; examples/z3/02-blame.bl — when the checker says no, it shows you the state.

; ── what you need to know first ──────────────────────────────────────────────
; `verify-process` (priv/lib/system/core.bl) takes a machine — a `defserver` with
; an `^{:invariant …}` annotation — and asks z3 a Hoare question for every
; transition:

;     is there a state where the invariant holds, the guard lets the call through,
;     the post-state is what the body computes, and the invariant is then FALSE?

; Read the polarity carefully, because everything below follows from it:

;     UNSAT  → no such state exists           → the step PRESERVES the invariant
;     SAT    → here is such a state           → the step BREAKS it, and the model
;                                              IS the state

; So a refutation is not a wall, it is a WITNESS: z3 hands back concrete values.
; Before this subfeature the verdict arrived as one bit (`holds: false`), which
; is not something you can act on. Now a refused step carries its counterexample:
;     counterexample: amt = 1, balance = 0, balance2 = -1
; — the shortest reproduction of the bug, in the machine's own variable names.

; The BEAM part: `verify-process` holds ONE conversation (`session`) with a leased
; z3 for the whole machine, and the counterexample costs one extra question inside
; that already-open conversation — asked only after a refusal. A green machine
; pays nothing. (Same shape as asking a supervisor which child failed, then asking
; that child for its last state while it is still running.)
; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(ns blame
  (:require [system.core :as sys]
            [z3]))

(defn- read1 [s] (first (BeamLisp.Reader/read_string s)))

;; ── 1. A guard that is too weak ──────────────────────────────────────────────
;; `:when (>= amt 0)` admits a withdrawal larger than the balance, so the
;; invariant `balance >= 0` is broken by a step the guard allows. This is the
;; ordinary bug, and the counterexample is the ordinary proof of it.
(def too-weak
  "(defserver ^{:invariant (>= balance 0)} bank
     (init [balance] (ok balance))
     (handle-call [:withdraw amt] [_ balance] :when (>= amt 0)
       (reply :ok (- balance amt))))")

;; ── 2. A next-state expression that is simply wrong ──────────────────────────
;; No guard at all, and the body computes a negative balance every time.
(def wrong-next
  "(defserver ^{:invariant (>= balance 0)} leaky
     (init [balance] (ok balance))
     (handle-call [:drain] [_ balance]
       (reply :ok (- balance 1))))")

(defn- show [title src]
  (let [r (sys/verify-process (z3/open) (read1 src))]
    (println "──" title)
    (println "   holds:" (:holds r) "  undecided:" (:undecided r))
    ;; `vec`, because `map` is LAZY in beam-lisp: without forcing it, nothing
    ;; inside the fn runs and the example prints an empty list (measured).
    (vec (map (fn [s]
                (let [w (:counterexample s)]
                  (println "   step" (:label s) "→" (:sentence w))))
              (:failures r)))))

(z3/pool! 2)
(show "a guard that is too weak" too-weak)
(show "a next-state that is wrong" wrong-next)

; ── what just happened ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;   a guard that is too weak  → counterexample: amt = 1, balance = 0, balance2 = -1
;   a next-state that is wrong → counterexample: balance = 0, balance2 = -1
;
; Both are minimal, both are in the machine's own names, and both are checkable by
; hand: take `balance = 0`, withdraw `amt = 1`, and `balance2 = -1` violates
; `balance >= 0`. The second machine needs no input at all — every drain breaks it.
;
; ── the other lane, and why it is NOT this one ───────────────────────────────
; `oracle/explain` exists too, and it reads an UNSAT CORE — the names of the
; assertions that are jointly unsatisfiable. It is the right tool when the expected
; answer is UNSAT (a synthesis or a repair search: `is there a guard that fixes
; this?` — `unsat` means no, and the core says what made it impossible).
;
; Using it here would be asking z3 for the core of a `sat` answer, which is asking
; for something it does not have. That is not a subtlety — it was measured: the
; first version of this file asked for a core after a refutation, and the answer
; came back `sat` with no core, which the runtime reported honestly as
; `no-core: the conversation is not in the state the refusal came from`.
;
; Where to look:
;   priv/lib/system/core.bl   step-obligation — the three named parts (g_guard,
;                             g_next, g_inv2), which is what makes a core readable
;                             at all; the refuted branch that asks for the model
;   priv/lib/oracle.bl        witness/2 — a refutation's counterexample
;                             explain/1 — a refusal's unsat core (the dual lane)
;   priv/std/z3pool.bl        the lease arms :produce-unsat-cores, so any
;                             conversation can be asked for a core
